      "text": "First, we simplify the expression \\((2+i)^4 + (2+i)^3 + (2+i)^2 + (2+i) + 1\\).\n\nStarting from the bottom:\n\\[\n(2+i)^2 = 4 + 4i + i^2 = 4 + 4i - 1 = 3 + 4i.\n\\]\nNext, we compute \\((2+i)^3\\):\n\\[\n(2+i)^3 = (2+i)(3+4i) = 6 + 8i + 3i + 4i^2 = 6 + 11i - 4 = 2 + 11i.\n\\]\nThen, we compute \\((2+i)^4\\):\n\\[\n(2+i)^4 = (2+i)(2+11i) = 4 + 22i + 2i + 11i^2 = 4 + 24i - 11 = -7 + 24i.\n\\]\nNow we substitute these back into the original expression:\n\\[\n-7 + 24i + 2 + 11i + 3 + 4i + 1 + 2i + 1 = -7 + 2 + 3 + 1 + 24i + 11i + 4i + 2i = 0 + 41i = 41i.\n\\]\nSo, the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\na(41i) + b(2+i)^3 + c(2+i)^2 + b(2+i) + a = 0.\n\\]\nFactoring out the common terms:\n\\[\na \\cdot 41i + b(2+i)^3 + c(3+4i) + b(2+i) + a = 0.\n\\]\nCombining like terms:\n\\[\na(41i) + b(2+i)^3 + c(3+4i) + b(2+i) + a = 0.\n\\]\nWe know that \\(2+i\\) is a root of the polynomial:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x + 5.\n\\]\nThus, \\(2+i\\) and \\(2-i\\) are roots. So, the polynomial \\(x^2 - 4x + 5\\) can be factored as:\n\\[\n(x - (2+i))(x - (2-i)) = x^2 - 4x + 5.\n\\]\nThis implies that:\n\\[\n(2+i) + (2-i) = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (2+i)(2-i) = 4.\n\\]\nSince the polynomial has integer coefficients, the polynomial must have real coefficients. Therefore, the sum of the coefficients must be real. The sum of the coefficients is:\n\\[\na(41i) + b(2)^2 + b(1)^2 = a(41i) + 4b + b = (4b + a(41i)) + b = b(4 + a) + a(41i).\n\\]\nFor this to be real, the imaginary part must be zero, so \\(41a + 4b = 0\\). Solving for \\(b\\):\n\\[\n4b = -41a \\implies b = -\\frac{41a}{4}.\n\\]\nSince \\(b\\) must be an integer, \\(41a\\) must be divisible by 4, so \\(a\\) must be divisible by 4. Let \\(a = 4k\\), then \\(b = -41k\\). The greatest common divisor of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) is 1, and \\(c\\) must be an integer. We need to find the absolute value of \\(c\\).\n\nTo find \\(c\\), we can use the fact that the polynomial must have integer coefficients. Since the sum of the coefficients is real, the coefficients of the imaginary parts must cancel out. Therefore, the coefficients of \\(i\\) must be zero:\n\\[\n41a = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4b = 0.\n\\]\nThis means \\(a = 0\\) or \\(b = 0\\), but since \\(a = 4k\\) and \\(b = -41k\\), both must be zero. Therefore, \\(a = b = 0\\). The polynomial simplifies to:\n\\[\nc(3+4i) + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 0.\n\\]\nThis implies \\(c = 0\\). Therefore, the absolute value of \\(c\\) is:\n\\[\n|c| = 0.\n\\]\nThe answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}.\n\\]",
