      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We begin by simplifying the expression $(2+i)^4 + (2+i)^3 + (2+i)^2 + (2+i)$. Using the binomial theorem and algebraic manipulation techniques, we can expand each term and then combine like terms to get a simplified expression.\n\nFirst, we calculate $(2+i)^2$:\n\\[\n(2+i)^2 = 4 + 4i + i^2 = 4 + 4i - 1 = 3 + 4i\n\\]\nNext, we calculate $(2+i)^3$:\n\\[\n(2+i)^3 = (2+i)(3+4i) = 6 + 8i + 3i + 4i^2 = 6 + 11i + 4(-1) = 6 + 11i - 4 = 2 + 11i\n\\]\nThen, we calculate $(2+i)^4$:\n\\[\n(2+i)^4 = (2+i)(2+11i) = 4 + 22i + 2i + 11i^2 = 4 + 24i + 11(-1) = 4 + 24i - 11 = -7 + 24i\n\\]\nNow we substitute these back into the original expression:\n\\[\na(-7 + 24i) + b(2 + 11i) + c(3 + 4i) + b(2 + i) + a\n\\]\nWe combine the real and imaginary parts separately:\n\\[\na(-7 + 24i) + b(-7i) + b(2) + b(i) + c(3) + c(4i) + a = -7a + b \\cdot 2 - 7bi + b \\cdot i + 3c + 4ci + a\n\\]\nSimplifying further, we get:\n\\[\n(-7a + a) + (-7b + b) + (-7bi + bi + 4ci) + 3c = -6a - 6bi + (4c + 3b)i\n\\]\nFor the expression to be zero, both the real and imaginary parts must be zero. Therefore, we have the system of equations:\n\\[\n-6a - 6b = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4c + 3b = 0\n\\]\nFrom the first equation, we get:\n\\[\n-6a - 6b = 0 \\implies a = -b\n\\]\nFrom the second equation, we get:\n\\[\n4c + 3b = 0 \\implies 4c = -3b \\implies c = -\\frac{3b}{4}\n\\]\nSince $a, b, c$ are integers and the greatest common divisor of $a, b, c$ is 1, $b$ must be a multiple of 4 for $c$ to be an integer. Let $b = 4k$ for some integer $k$. Then $a = -4k$ and $c = -3k$. The greatest common divisor of $a, b, c$ is $k$, so $k = 1$ (since the gcd must be 1). Therefore, $b = 4$, $a = -4$, and $c = -3$. The greatest absolute value of $c$ is:\n\\[\n|c| = |c| = |-3| = 3\n\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
